- op: discuss  # 7e04
  at: '2026-08-26T01:09:02Z'  # 7e04
  by: agent  # 7e04
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7e04
  clock: 4  # 7e04
  nonce: '7e04'  # 7e04
  message: "DECISION RECORDED BY THE AGENT, not the owner -- reversible, reasoning below.\n\nThis item says \"MEASURE BEFORE CHOOSING (b): size how many of the 13 file-anchor rows would actually gain a dependence, rather than assuming module-scope walks would confirm them.\" Measurement 0006 supplies that sizing on a defended frame.\n\n(a) IS DONE. The publish half shipped in PR #533 (docs/VERIFY-CLAIMS-SCOPE.md), including the section-3a intraprocedural limit with a measured share. Nothing further is owed on (a).\n\n(b) IS DECIDED: DO NOT BUILD. Two independent reasons, and the second is the one that surprised me.\n\nREASON 1 -- under CONFIRM-ONLY section 3a, a wider walk cannot improve precision. Inclusion is decided by `adjudicated = walk(...) is True`; a walk that runs and returns False or None changes the LABEL and nothing else. So widening the walk to module scope moves findings from `structural` to `ddg_mixed` without removing any. The only behavioural effect available today is the PR #214 path -- a False plus a same-function sanitizer earns `sanitized`, which DROPS a flow. That is a RECALL change, taken during a period when the band ruling has recall work stopped. This item already flagged that direction risk; 0006 does not retire it.\n\nREASON 2 -- (b) targets the HEALTHIEST population in the sample. On 0006, source-symbol kind splits:\n\n  function   n=78  TP=23 FP=55  precision 29.5%\n  file       n=32  TP=15 FP=17  precision 46.9%\n  method     n= 2  TP= 0 FP= 2  precision  0.0%\n\nFile-anchored sources are 32 of 112 (29%) and ALL 32 are `structural`, exactly as this item predicts by construction. But their precision is 46.9%, well ABOVE the function-anchored 29.5%. The false-positive mass lives in function-anchored sources -- 55 of 74 FPs. So (b) would spend real implementation effort widening the walk into the segment that is already performing best, while the segment the walk ALREADY covers is where the errors are.\n\nWHAT WOULD REOPEN IT. Two conditions, both required: (i) the section 7a chain lands -- INV (the ddg_mixed False/None conflation) then WI-kabif -- so that a walk returning False can actually remove a finding rather than only relabel it; and (ii) a later measurement shows file-anchored precision at or below function-anchored, which would mean the FP mass has moved into the segment (b) targets. Until both hold, (b) is effort aimed away from the problem.\n\nCAVEAT ON THE NUMBERS: n=32 file-anchored on one cohort, and 0006 is N=1 under a new frame. The 46.9%-vs-29.5% gap is wide enough to act on but has not been reproduced."  # 7e04
